    <system:String x:Key="Speed_HintSata2">• About 270 MB/s is the limit of a SATA 3 Gb/s port or an old cable. Plug the SSD into a SATA 6 Gb/s port (usually SATA0/SATA1 on the motherboard).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Speed_HintUsb2">• About 40 MB/s is the limit of USB 2.0. Use a blue (USB 3) port and the drive's own cable.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Speed_HintPcie">• Much slower than its rating: the M.2 slot may run at an older PCIe generation or fewer lanes, or the SSD is overheating (add a heatsink).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Speed_HintPcieGen3">• Reads stop at about {0:N0} MB/s although this model is rated {1:N0} MB/s: that is the limit of a PCIe 3.0 x4 slot. Many boards have one M.2 slot wired to PCIe 4.0 (usually the one nearest the CPU) — move the SSD there to get its full speed.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Speed_HintFull">• Only {0:0.#}% of this SSD is free ({1:0.#} GB). A nearly full SSD loses its fast write cache, so writes slow down a lot. Free up space with Cleaner or Disk Space, then test again.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Speed_HintDips">• The curve has {0:N0} sudden dips. On a hard disk that can mean weak or remapped sectors — back up important files and check its health (SMART).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Speed_HintSsdSlow">• {0:N0} places read at less than half speed — often old data on a TLC/QLC SSD; a firmware update can help.</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Speed_HintAccess">• Random access takes {0:0.0} ms — slower than a healthy hard disk (about 12–20 ms).</system:String>
    <system:String x:Key="Speed_HintGeneric">• Other programs using the drive, a nearly full SSD, power-saving mode or heat can all lower the numbers. Close other programs and test again.</system:String>
