    <system:String x:Key="Health_ConfirmMark" xml:space="preserve">Mark the bad sectors on {0} so Windows never uses them again?&#x0a;&#x0a;Windows' own check (chkdsk /r) reads every cluster of the drive, moves whatever can still be read to healthy space and records the bad ones in the file system's bad-cluster list. It takes a long time (hours on a large hard disk).&#x0a;&#x0a;• A data drive is locked while it runs: programs with files open on it lose them — save and close them first.&#x0a;• The Windows drive is checked at the next restart.&#x0a;&#x0a;Back up your important files first if you can.</system:String>
